WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.720
Birds eat more dark moths than pale ones on these trees.

2
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.160
Is that evolution?

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.140
Not yet. This film builds the answer one piece at a time.

4
00:00:16.133 --> 00:00:17.473
Meet the peppered moth.

5
00:00:18.773 --> 00:00:20.993
It comes in two main colour morphs.

6
00:00:21.873 --> 00:00:24.433
A morph is one distinct form within a species.

7
00:00:25.753 --> 00:00:27.393
The pale morph is called typica.

8
00:00:28.613 --> 00:00:30.573
The dark morph is called carbonaria.

9
00:00:31.953 --> 00:00:35.013
Intermediate moths exist too, but we follow these two.

10
00:00:36.373 --> 00:00:38.073
Both rest by day on tree trunks.

11
00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:41.057
Birds hunt these moths by sight.

12
00:00:42.297 --> 00:00:45.737
On a trunk covered in pale lichen, the dark morph stands out.

13
00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:49.477
So birds find more dark moths than pale ones.

14
00:00:50.677 --> 00:00:51.877
The bird is the agent.

15
00:00:53.037 --> 00:00:55.357
The lichen-covered trunk is the environment.

16
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.460
Fitness is reproductive success compared with other variants.

17
00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.560
Here we measure one part of it, survival through a day.

18
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.300
A dial shows the ratio: dark survival divided by pale survival.

19
00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.700
On lichen, the dial sits below one.

20
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.100
Fitness is relative.

21
00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:21.460
What matters is how one morph does compared with another in the same place.

22
00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:24.943
Now change the environment, not the moths.

23
00:01:26.223 --> 00:01:30.983
In the 19th century, air pollution killed the lichens around British industrial

24
00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:33.883
cities. Soot coated the bark.

25
00:01:35.143 --> 00:01:37.763
On dark bark, the pale morph stands out instead.

26
00:01:39.003 --> 00:01:42.623
The dial swings above one, and the dark morph became common there.

27
00:01:43.633 --> 00:01:48.493
Then, in the late 20th century, air pollution fell and the lichens grew back.

28
00:01:49.753 --> 00:01:51.873
Which morph do the birds now take more often?

29
00:01:58.400 --> 00:01:59.680
The dark morph again.

30
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.140
It stands out on lichen, so the dial swings back below one, and carbonaria declined.

31
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:08.860
Same moth, same birds.

32
00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.580
A different environment favoured a different colour.

33
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.920
Fitness belongs to the moth in a place.

34
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.163
Notice what the birds did not do.

35
00:02:20.283 --> 00:02:22.323
They did not make any dark or pale moths.

36
00:02:23.603 --> 00:02:26.503
Like a sieve, they sorted moths that already existed.

37
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:30.783
That sorting, within one generation, is selection.

38
00:02:32.023 --> 00:02:34.103
A drought does not create deep beaks either.

39
00:02:35.303 --> 00:02:36.983
It removes more of the shallow ones.

40
00:02:36.983 --> 00:02:37.983
. . . .

41
00:02:37.983 --> 00:02:39.503
Is it evolution yet?

42
00:02:40.783 --> 00:02:42.923
Only if offspring resemble their parents.

43
00:02:44.403 --> 00:02:45.403
Picture two futures.

44
00:02:46.603 --> 00:02:51.263
In one, colour is inherited, and the young of the survivors can carry some of the

45
00:02:51.263 --> 00:02:56.983
shift. In the other, colour is not inherited, so the shift need not pass to the

46
00:02:56.983 --> 00:02:57.983
young.

47
00:02:58.267 --> 00:03:00.967
So a full selection argument has four parts.

48
00:03:02.387 --> 00:03:06.767
Variation, inheritance, a fitness difference, and an agent in an environment.

49
00:03:08.147 --> 00:03:10.007
An agent sorts variants by fitness.

50
00:03:11.407 --> 00:03:13.427
Inheritance carries the sorting forward.
